          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child with anaemia has raised unconjugated bilirubin, raised LDH, low haptoglo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child with anaemia has raised unconjugated bilirubin, raised LDH, low haptoglobin, and a high reticulocyte count. Which mechanism of anaemia do these laboratory findings indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Decreased red blood cell production</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Decreased production (hypoproliferative anaemia) would show a low reticulocyte count, since the marrow is failing to generate new red cells. The high reticulocyte count here indicates the marrow is responding to cell loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increased red blood cell destruction</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. This combination — raised bilirubin from haem breakdown, raised LDH from cell lysis, low haptoglobin (consumed by binding free haemoglobin), and high reticulocytes (marrow compensation) — is the classic laboratory signature of haemolysis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic blood loss</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic blood loss can raise reticulocytes, but it does not typically raise LDH or lower haptoglobin, since red cells are lost intact rather than lysed. The bilirubin and LDH pattern here points to red cell destruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow suppression by leukaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Marrow suppression causes a low reticulocyte count with anaemia, since the marrow cannot mount a compensatory response. The high reticulocytes here rule out marrow failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the three mechanisms: decreased production shows low reticulocytes; haemolysis shows raised bilirubin, raised LDH, low haptoglobin, and high reticulocytes; blood loss shows high reticulocytes but without the haemolysis markers."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Recognising the haemolysis lab pattern — raised bilirubin, raised LDH, low haptoglobin, high reticulocytes — lets you classify the mechanism before considering specific diagnoses."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
