          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An eight-month-old exclusively breastfed infant of a strict vegetarian mother pr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An eight-month-old exclusively breastfed infant of a strict vegetarian mother presents with pallor, developmental regression, and hypotonia. Investigations show macrocytic anaemia. Which deficiency is most likely responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Iron deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Iron deficiency causes microcytic anaemia (low MCV), not macrocytic. This child has macrocytic anaemia with neurological features, pointing to a different cause.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Folate deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Folate deficiency causes macrocytic anaemia but does NOT produce neurological features. The developmental regression and hypotonia here indicate vitamin B12 deficiency specifically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vitamin B12 deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Vitamin B12 deficiency uniquely causes both macrocytic anaemia and neurological dysfunction — hypotonia, developmental regression, and poor interaction in infants. A breastfed baby of a vegetarian mother is the classic at-risk profile in India.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vitamin C deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes bleeding gums, petechiae, and bone pain — not macrocytic anaemia or developmental regression. The macrocytic picture with neurological signs points to B12 deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: both B12 and folate deficiency cause macrocytic anaemia, but only B12 deficiency produces neurological features (hypotonia, developmental regression, loss of vibration sense). That distinction is clinically decisive."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. In Indian practice, developmental regression in a breastfed infant of a vegetarian mother should immediately raise suspicion for B12 deficiency, even before blood results return."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
