          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, which clinical feature results from extramedul</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, which clinical feature results from extramedullary infiltration by leukaemic cells rather than from bone marrow failure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pallor and fatigue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pallor and fatigue result from anaemia due to reduced red cell production — a direct consequence of marrow failure, not organ infiltration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Recurrent fever and infections</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recurrent infections result from neutropenia caused by marrow failure — leukaemic blasts crowd out normal granulocyte precursors, reducing the body's ability to fight infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone pain and hepatosplenomegaly</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bone pain occurs when leukaemic cells infiltrate the periosteum, and hepatosplenomegaly results from leukaemic infiltration of the liver and spleen. These are extramedullary manifestations, distinct from the cytopenias caused by marrow failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Petechiae and mucosal bleeding</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bleeding manifestations result from thrombocytopenia — reduced platelet production due to marrow failure — not from organ infiltration by leukaemic cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In ALL, marrow failure causes anaemia (pallor), thrombocytopenia (bleeding), and neutropenia (infections). Organ infiltration causes hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, bone pain, and CNS or testicular involvement. Separating these two mechanisms is key."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Understanding ALL's dual pathophysiology — marrow failure causing cytopenias versus organ infiltration causing hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, and bone pain — helps you predict and explain the full clinical picture."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
