          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child has microcytic anaemia with a low MCV and a normal red cell distribution</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child has microcytic anaemia with a low MCV and a normal red cell distribution width (RDW). Which condition does this pattern most likely indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Iron deficiency anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In iron deficiency anaemia, the RDW is characteristically elevated because iron-depleted red cells vary widely in size (anisocytosis). A normal RDW with microcytosis points away from IDA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thalassaemia trait</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In thalassaemia trait, the red cells are uniformly small, so the MCV is low but the RDW remains normal. This contrasts with iron deficiency, where cells vary in size and the RDW is high — a key diagnostic shortcut before ordering haemoglobin electrophoresis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Folate deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Folate deficiency causes macrocytic anaemia (high MCV), not microcytic. A low MCV immediately rules out folate deficiency as the cause.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aplastic anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Aplastic anaemia typically presents with normocytic or mildly macrocytic red cells and pancytopenia (all three cell lines low), not isolated microcytosis with a normal RDW.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the shortcut: in iron deficiency, cells vary widely in size (high RDW); in thalassaemia trait, cells are uniformly small (normal RDW). Both have a low MCV, but the RDW separates them."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Low MCV + normal RDW = uniformly small cells = thalassaemia trait. Low MCV + high RDW = variably sized cells = iron deficiency. This distinction guides your next investigation."
                }
              ],
